{
  "dom_id": "step-q344894",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/bd78704be61e30879d4e659a255f93f5c8b6e560951d06b23cf547c8846b01ee.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{\u2032}(2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{\u2032}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(x)={f}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(2)={f}^{\u2032}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $h(2)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $2.$ So, ${h}^{\u2032}(2)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{\u2032}(2) & ={f}^{\u2032}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 0+(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
